{"findings":[
{"rule":"K3","where":"Deployment._deployClaimIssuer (override calls ctr.removeClaimTopic(KYC_TOPIC) after super added it)","severity":"HIGH","why":"The override removes the only required claim topic, so the ClaimTopicsRegistry is empty at the end of setup. No KYC claim topic is required, which violates the rule that at least one must be.","steps":"setUp -> super._deployClaimIssuer adds topic 1 -> override removes topic 1 -> ctr.getClaimTopics() returns []"}, 
{"rule":"K1","where":"IdentityRegistry.isVerified with an empty required-topics list (caused by the setup override)","severity":"HIGH","why":"With no required topics, isVerified returns true for any wallet that is merely registered, with no claim or trusted issuer check. A registered investor with no KYC claim can therefore receive tokens.","steps":"agent registers wallet m (idM, no claim issued) -> agent calls token.mint(m, 100) succeeds -> a holder calls token.transfer(m, 10) succeeds"}, 
{"rule":"K2","where":"IdentityRegistry.isVerified with an empty required-topics list (caused by the setup override)","severity":"HIGH","why":"Revoking a claim has no effect on verification because claims are never checked. A wallet whose KYC was revoked can still receive tokens.","steps":"_revokeClaim(idB) -> a holder calls token.transfer(b, 10) still succeeds because isVerified(b) returns true"}
]}
